---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import products from "../../data/products_launch.json";
import paints from "../../data/paints_launch.json";
import { OUTCOME_FILTERS, glareScoreFromIndex } from "../../lib/match.js";
import { materialGroupClash } from "../../lib/format.js";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const description =
  "How the Eudaspace directory reads Kelvin, CRI, flicker, acoustics, VOC, LRV and glare. Unknowns stay Not published. Evidence notes are quoted from the launch files.";

const notes = new Map();
for (const product of products) {
  const note = (product.evidence_note || "").trim();
  if (!notes.has(note)) notes.set(note, []);
  notes.get(note).push(product.name);
}

const glareRows = [...new Map(paints.map((paint) => [paint.glare_score, paint.glare_band])).entries()]
  .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0])
  .map(([score, band]) => {
    const group = paints.filter((paint) => paint.glare_score === score);
    const indexes = group.map((paint) => paint.glare_index);
    const lrvs = group.map((paint) => paint.lrv);
    return {
      score,
      band,
      count: group.length,
      indexMin: Math.min(...indexes),
      indexMax: Math.max(...indexes),
      lrvMin: Math.min(...lrvs),
      lrvMax: Math.max(...lrvs),
    };
  });

const psychBases = [...new Set(paints.map((paint) => paint.psych_basis))];
const orientationBases = [...new Set(paints.map((paint) => paint.orientation_basis))];
const clashes = products.filter((product) => materialGroupClash(product.material_group, product.material));
const maxGap = Math.max(
  ...paints.map((paint) => Math.abs(paint.lrv * (1 + paint.sheen_pct / 20) - paint.glare_index)),
);
---

<Base title="How the specs are read | Eudaspace Calm Home Directory" description={description}>
  <article class="wrap page-intro method">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol class="crumb">
        <li><a href={base}>Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Methodology</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <p class="eyebrow">Science-cautious, on purpose</p>
    <h1>How I read these numbers</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is neurodesign, not interior design. The research behind light, sound and attention
      is real, and I would rather understate it than dress it up. You will not get an invented
      percentage or a wellness score for a hallway. You get the figure a maker published, the
      evidence note stored with it, and Not published where that is the honest gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pages are rendered from <code>products_launch</code> ({products.length} rows) and
      <code>paints_launch</code> ({paints.length} rows). The JSON and CSV copies of each file
      are both in the repo. I do not recalculate a spec and then hide the stored one.
    </p>

    <h2>What “Not published” means</h2>
    <p>
      A blank, a null, or the word “unknown” in the launch file is shown as Not published.
      I do not guess Kelvin, CRI, flicker, NRC, αw or a VOC certificate. The one VOC cell that
      says unknown and then mentions low-VOC adhesive advice is shown as Not published, with
      that note kept. It is not a certificate.
    </p>

    <h2>Outcome names</h2>
    <p>
      The product file uses five tags. The filters use plainer names for the same tags. The
      card still carries the filed wording for a screen reader.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Filter</th>
          <th scope="col">Filed as</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          OUTCOME_FILTERS.map((item) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{item.label}</td>
              <td>{item.source}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Light, sound, VOC and material</h2>
    <p>
      Kelvin filters keep a product when its published minimum-to-maximum range overlaps the
      band you pick. A dim-to-warm bulb can match more than one band. If Kelvin was not
      published, it only matches “Kelvin not published”.
    </p>
    <p>
      CRI is shown only when the file has a number. The flicker filter called “under 5%” matches
      the rows whose flicker cell contains “&lt;5%”. Some of those makers also say flicker-free.
      A cell that only says unknown is not treated as flicker-free.
    </p>
    <p>
      Acoustic filters use NRC and αw. Almost every product has neither. I do not turn “not
      applicable” into a zero.
    </p>
    <p>
      Material filters use the material string. The material group is also printed, as filed.
      {clashes.length === 0
        ? "No material-group clash is in this file."
        : `${clashes.length} products are filed under Glass / LED while the material string describes something else. I have not corrected the file.`}
      {clashes.length > 0 && (
        <span>
          {" "}
          Those products are {clashes.map((product) => product.name).join("; ")}.
        </span>
      )}
    </p>

    <h2>Paint: LRV, orientation, effect</h2>
    <p>
      LRV on the card is the number in the file. The source and confidence are printed with it.
      LRV bands in the filter (under 20, 20 to under 40, and so on) are cuts I use so you can
      narrow the list. They are not names the paint makers publish.
    </p>
    <p>Orientation is stored in two ways, and the card quotes the basis it was given:</p>
    <ul>
      {orientationBases.map((basis) => <li>{basis}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      A north, south, east or west filter matches a shade when that aspect is one of the
      aspects in its orientation cell. Several shades are tagged for more than one aspect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Psychological effect is an editorial tag. The file says so. I am not treating these as
      clinical effects. The bases in this launch file are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {psychBases.map((basis) => <li>{basis}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Glare score method</h2>
    <p>
      Glare score is not a measured disability-glare result, and it is not a British Standard.
      It is a figure stored beside each shade so these thirty can be compared.
    </p>
    <p>
      For every shade, <code>sheen_pct</code> is 3. The file says that 3% is assumed for a
      flat-matt class, because the maker’s sheen percentage was not captured. I have not
      replaced that assumption with a measured gloss.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stored glare index tracks LRV × (1 + sheen_pct / 20). At 3% sheen that factor is
      1.15, so the index sits on LRV × 1.15. Across this file the largest gap between the
      stored index and that product is {maxGap.toFixed(3)}, which is one rounding step at one
      decimal place. On an exact half, the stored tenth can sit 0.1 either side of a
      round-half choice. The card shows the stored glare index, not a number I recalculate.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score from 1 to 5, and the band name, are stored too. In this file they line up with
      the index if you cut it at 22, 40, 65 and 82:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Under 22: score 1, Very low</li>
      <li>22 to under 40: score 2, Low</li>
      <li>40 to under 65: score 3, Moderate</li>
      <li>65 to under 82: score 4, Higher</li>
      <li>82 and over: score 5, Highest</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Those cuts reproduce every score in the file. They sit in the gaps between neighbouring
      shades. They are a reading of this file, not a threshold I found in a lighting standard.
      A higher score means a lighter surface on this index, and so more chance of glare. It
      does not tell you that a room will be uncomfortable.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Score</th>
          <th scope="col">Band</th>
          <th scope="col">Shades</th>
          <th scope="col">Glare index in file</th>
          <th scope="col">LRV in file</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          glareRows.map((row) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{row.score}</td>
              <td>{row.band}</td>
              <td>{row.count}</td>
              <td>
                {row.indexMin}–{row.indexMax}
              </td>
              <td>
                {row.lrvMin}–{row.lrvMax}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      I checked that those cuts match the stored score for every shade
      {paints.every((paint) => glareScoreFromIndex(paint.glare_index) === paint.glare_score)
        ? " (they do, in this launch file)."
        : "."}
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence notes</h2>
    <p>
      These are the evidence notes stored on the products, quoted, with the products they
      belong to. Where the file has no note, I have not written one.
    </p>
    {
      [...notes.entries()].map(([note, names]) => (
        <section>
          {note ? <blockquote class="evidence">{note}</blockquote> : <p>Not published.</p>}
          <p class="note">{names.join("; ")}</p>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <h2>What this is not</h2>
    <p>
      This is not medical, sleep-clinic or diagnostic advice. Sleep, attention and cortisol
      appear only where an evidence note in the file mentions them, and those notes are
      cautious on purpose. Prices are the crawl prices in the file, not a live shop. Sample
      links use the sample URL in the file. If that note says no sample variant was found, the
      card says so, even when the URL is the shade page. Affiliate programme names in the data
      files are not turned into tracking links.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
